{
  "version": 1,
  "speakingPromptVersion": 1,
  "passages": [
    {"id":"reading-a1","band":"A1","text":"Mia works in a small cafe. She starts at eight in the morning. At lunchtime, she eats a sandwich in the park next to the cafe."},
    {"id":"reading-a2","band":"A2","text":"The community library will close early on Friday because the staff are attending a training session. Books due on Friday may be returned on Saturday without a fine. The online catalogue will remain available."},
    {"id":"reading-b1","band":"B1","text":"When Leo moved closer to his office, he expected to save time. His commute did become shorter, but the neighborhood shops were more expensive. He now cooks most meals at home, so his monthly spending has actually fallen."},
    {"id":"reading-b2","band":"B2","text":"The city introduced shared bicycles to reduce short car journeys. Early figures show heavy use near train stations, yet limited use in outer districts. Officials argue that adding safer cycle lanes, rather than simply more bicycles, will determine whether the scheme changes travel habits."},
    {"id":"reading-c1","band":"C1","text":"Remote work was initially praised for eliminating the commute, but its longer-term effects are less straightforward. Employees often value autonomy while also reporting that informal learning has become harder. The central challenge is therefore not choosing between office and home, but designing routines that preserve flexibility without weakening collaboration."},
    {"id":"reading-c2","band":"C2","text":"Predictions about technology frequently mistake visibility for significance: a dramatic invention attracts attention, whereas gradual changes in habits may transform society more profoundly. This does not make forecasting futile, but it does suggest that forecasters should examine ordinary behavior alongside technical novelty and remain cautious about confident timelines."}
  ],
  "speakingPrompts": [
    {"id":"speaking-1","sequenceIndex":0,"text":"Describe a place or activity you enjoy. Explain what it is like and why you enjoy it. You may use a fictional example if you prefer."},
    {"id":"speaking-2","sequenceIndex":1,"text":"Tell us about an experience in which a plan changed. Explain what happened, how you responded, and what the result was. The experience may be imaginary."},
    {"id":"speaking-3","sequenceIndex":2,"text":"Some people prefer learning alone, while others prefer learning with a group. Give your opinion, support it with reasons, and consider one advantage of the other view."}
  ],
  "questions": [
